

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — tracking inside [38;5;153mlogin()[39m. Next question, which is the one that shapes the module's interface.

  [1mThe fork:[0m where a tracking record goes once [38;5;153mlogin()[39m creates it. This determines whether the tracking module is a
  one-function file or something with a configurable sink, and it's the part that's awkward to change later because
  every call to it is written against that shape.

  - [1mConsole only[0m — records format and print, matching how the rest of this fixture stubs things out. Buys the smallest
    honest module and zero new concepts. Costs nothing now; if you later want records sent somewhere, you change one
    function's body, not its callers. Cheap to change.
  - [1mConsole now, pluggable sink from the start[0m — the module takes a sink function, defaulting to console. Buys a seam
    for a future backend and makes the module testable without capturing stdout. Costs one extra concept in a codebase
    that currently has none. Still cheap to change.
  - [1mPOST to an endpoint[0m — records go to a tracking URL alongside [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. Buys real tracking. Costs error handling,
    async, and a decision about what happens when the tracking call fails but the login succeeded — tracking must never
    break login, which is real design work. Hardest to undo.

  [1mMy recommendation: console only.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m itself is still a stub that never touches [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m; building a delivery
  mechanism for records produced by a fake login is committing to plumbing ahead of the thing it serves. YAGNI says
  start with the record and its shape — that's the part you'll keep — and add delivery when there's a real login to
  track. The pluggable sink is a reasonable second choice if you want the test seam now.
